# 2. Software Requirements Specification

## 2.1 Functional Requirements

### FR1 — Candidate Management

The system shall allow candidate information to be created and retrieved.

Candidate information includes:

- User ID
- First name
- Last name
- Email
- Password
- Location

### FR2 — Qualifications

The system shall support qualifications associated with candidates.

A qualification contains:

- Qualification ID
- Qualification name
- Institution
- Year obtained
- Candidate ID

### FR3 — Work Experience

The system shall support work experience associated with candidates.

### FR4 — References

The system shall support candidate references.

### FR5 — Organisations

The system shall support organisations that create or own job opportunities.

### FR6 — Jobs

The system shall support jobs containing:

- Job ID
- Title
- Description
- Location
- Requirements
- Status
- Organisation

### FR7 — Applications

The system shall support applications connecting candidates to jobs.

Application statuses include:

- PENDING
- UNDER_REVIEW
- ACCEPTED
- REJECTED

### FR8 — Verification

The system shall evaluate candidate information through the verification workflow and produce a verification result and reason.

### FR9 — Appeals

The system shall allow an application that has been rejected to have an associated appeal.

### FR10 — REST API

The system shall expose REST endpoints for supported application operations.

Current API endpoints include:

- `GET /health`
- `GET /candidates/{userId}`
- `POST /candidates`

## 2.2 Non-Functional Requirements

### Performance

The application should respond to normal API requests within a reasonable time under expected prototype usage.

### Security

- Database credentials must not be hard-coded for deployment.
- Passwords and secrets must be supplied through environment variables.
- Database access should be restricted through appropriate network rules in cloud deployment.

### Maintainability

The project should use separate model, service, repository, controller, and DTO responsibilities where appropriate.

### Reliability

Automated tests should be maintained as the application evolves.

### Portability

The application should be runnable locally and inside a Docker container.

## 2.3 Constraints

- The project is an educational prototype.
- External verification providers are not currently integrated.
- Production-grade authentication and authorisation are future work.
- The cloud architecture is being introduced incrementally.

## 2.4 Acceptance Criteria

The prototype will be considered successful when:

1. The project builds successfully with Maven.
2. Automated tests pass.
3. PostgreSQL connectivity works.
4. The Spring Boot application starts successfully.
5. Candidate API endpoints return expected responses.
6. The application can run inside Docker.
7. The cloud deployment can connect securely to its cloud PostgreSQL database.
